  "title": "SYSTEM AND METHOD FOR PRESENTATION OF COMPUTER VISION USING USER DATA",
  "technical_field": "[0002] This disclosure relates generally to computer vision and more particularly to systems and methods for presenting content associated with users.",
  "background_art": "[0003] Computer vision applications such as augmented reality (AR), mixed reality (MR), and/or virtual reality (VR) often require interaction between physical devices (e.g., smart phones, tablets, laptops, etc.) and real world environments. For example, AR/MR/VR experiences may utilize sensors (e.g., cameras, accelerometers, gyroscopes, proximity sensors, microphones, global positioning satellites, etc.) of a device to capture information about its environment and present it to a user. However, these interactions are typically limited to pre-programmed actions and do not allow for dynamic changes in response to changing conditions within the environment.
[0004] Various aspects of this disclosure provide techniques for enhancing computer vision experiences through the use of user data. In some examples, a mobile device can scan an image containing a scannable code (e.g., QR code). The scannable code can correspond to a unique user ID. When the mobile device connects with the corresponding user's social media profile, the user's posts, stories, and other relevant data can be displayed as a series of "bubbles" that appear to emanate from the scannable code. These bubbles can change over time based on new posts made by the user.
[0005] According to various embodiments, a computer-implemented method of generating an augmented reality experience is described herein. The method can include receiving, at a computing device, a request to access a user interface of a computer program. The computer program can be configured to facilitate communication among users of the computer program via a network. The method can also include identifying, via the computing device, a target location of a camera view of the computing device. The target location can be identified based on a captured frame of video stream data received from the camera view of the computing device. The method can also include detecting, via the computing device, a visual marker embedded in the captured frame of video stream data. The visual marker can be associated with a particular user of the computer program. The method can also include accessing, via the computing device, a digital representation of a post created by the particular user. The post can have been previously posted by the particular user on the computer program. The post can include text and/or multimedia content. The method can also include rendering, via the computing device, the digital representation of the post on top of the captured frame of video stream data, so that the rendered post appears as if the post were being emitted from the visual marker. The rendered post can include a three-dimensional animated representation of the text and/or the multimedia content included in the post. The three-dimensional animated representation can be generated using a combination of graphics processing unit (GPU) acceleration and physics-based simulations.
The method can also include determining, via the computing device, whether a subsequent post has been created by the particular user since the post was accessed. The method can also include updating, upon determining that the subsequent post has been created by the particular user, the digital representation of the post to reflect the subsequent post. Updating the digital representation of the post can include modifying the three-dimensional animated representation of the text and/or multimedia content included in the post. Modifying the three-dimensional animated representation of the text and multimedia content can include replacing the text and/or multimedia content of the post with the text and/or multimedia content of the subsequent post.
The method can also include displaying, via the computing device, a notification indicating that there is a subsequent post available from the particular user. The notification can be displayed on the user interface of the computer program. The notification can indicate that the subsequent post has been detected by the computing device.
The method can also include determining, via at least one processor of the computing device, whether the subsequent post is an ephemeral post. Ephemeral posts are temporary posts that automatically expire after a specified period of time. If the subsequent post is an ephemeral post, the method can include deleting, via the computing device, the subsequent post after expiration of the specified period of time. Deleting the subsequent post can prevent the subsequent post from appearing on the computer program after the expiration of the specified period of time.
The method can also include detecting, via the computing de-vice, another visual marker embedded in the captured frame of video steam data. The other visual marker can be associated with another user of the computer program. The method can include accessing, via the computing device, a different digital represen-tation of a different post created by the other user. The different post can have been previously posted by the other user on the computer program. The different post can include text and/or multimedia content. Rendering, via the computing device, the different digital representa-tion of the different post on top of the captured frame of video steam data, so that the rendered different post appears as if the different post were being emitted from the other visu-al marker. The rendered different post can include a three-dimen-sional animated representation of the text and/or the multi-media content included in the different post. The three-dimensional animated representation can be obtained using a combina-tion of GPU acceleration and physics-based simulations. Determin-ing, via the computing device, whether another subsequent post has been created by the other user since the different post was accessed. Updating, upon determining that the other subse-quent post has been created by the other user, the different digi-tal representation of the different post to reflect the other subse-quent post. Updating the different digital representation of the different post can include modifying the three-dimensional animat-ed representation of the text and/or multimedia content included therein. Modifying the three-dimensional animated representa-tion of the text and/or multimedia content can include replac-ing the text and/or multimedia content of the different post with the text and/or multimedia content of another subsequent post.
The method can also include displaying a notification indicat-ing that there is another subsequent post available from the other user. The notification can be displayed on the computer pro-gram's user interface. The notification can indicate that the other subsequent post has been detected by the computing device. Determining, via at least one processor of the computing devic-e, whether the other subsequent post is an ephemeral post. If the other subsequent post is an ephemeral post, the computed method can include deleting, via the computing device the other subsequent post after expiration of the specified period of times. Deleting the other subsequent post can prevent the other subsequent post from appearing on the computer program after expira-tion of the specified period of time.",
  "summary_of_invention": "[0010] Aspects of the present disclosure relate to systems and methods for enhanced computer vision experiences. Some aspects relate to providing computer vision capabilities that leverage user data. For instance, a device can identify features (e.g., scannable codes, barcodes, markers, stickers, etc.) in one or more images taken by the device. Based on the identification of the features, the device can retrieve additional information related to the features. This additional information can include user data, which can be used to create interactive augmented reality displays. For example, if the feature corresponds to a user account (e.g., Facebook account, Instagram account, Twitter account, etc.), the device can retrieve a list of posts, including photos, videos, audio files, and other types of content, from the user account. Using the retrieved user data, the device can generate one or more augmented reality effects, animations, or other graphical overlays that can be superimposed onto the original image(s).
[0011] By leveraging user data, the functionality of com-puter vision applications can be extended beyond what would otherwise be possible without such data. For instance, rather than simply allowing users to interact with a static scene, computer vision applications can now incorporate moving, evolving, or other dynamic components that are tied to specific users or events. Accordingly, computer vision applications can become even more immersive and engaging, enabling users to engage with their surroundings in ways they never thought possible before.
[0012] While certain embodiments are susceptible to various modifica-tions and alternative forms, specifics thereof will become apparent upon reviewing the following description and accompanying drawings. Additionally, each embodiment encompasses various obvious variations and modifications that may be discerned from those described herein by one skilled in the art based on a knowledge of the teachings contained herein. As used in this specification and the appended claims, the singular forms "a," "an," and "the" include plural referents unless the context clearly dictates otherwise.
[0013] Accordingly, the present invention includes all modifi-cations and equivalents of the subject matter recited in the appended claims. It should be noted that the figures are not necessarily drawn to scale. Rather, the dimensions of the various features may be exaggerated and/or simplified in the drawings to better understand the inventive concepts. Thus, the figures are illustrative and are not meant to be considered limiting.
