  "title": "SYSTEMS AND METHODS FOR OPTIMIZING VIDEO STREAMS IN THREE-DIMENSIONAL VIRTUAL ENVIRONMENTS",
  "technical_field": "[0001] This disclosure relates generally to systems and methods for managing video streams in three-dimensional (3D) environments.",
  "background_art": "[0002] Virtual reality (VR) technologies have evolved significantly over time, offering immersive experiences that transport users to new dimensions beyond the physical world. These advancements have enabled people to engage with digital spaces as if they were real, using specialized headsets and controllers to navigate through interactive environments. [0003] One key aspect of VR is its ability to create photorealistic representations of objects, characters, and settings, allowing users to interact with them as if they existed in the real world. However, this level of detail comes at a cost. High-quality visualizations require significant computational power, storage capacity, and network bandwidth, making it challenging to maintain smooth, seamless interactions without straining hardware capabilities.
[0004] Another challenge arises from the need to balance resource usage against the demands of delivering high-fidelity visuals. As VR applications become increasingly sophisticated, developers must carefully manage these trade-offs between graphical fidelity and system performance. If too much processing power is devoted to generating detailed graphics, other aspects of the experience may suffer, such as responsiveness, frame rates, and overall stability. Conversely, prioritizing efficiency might compromise the richness of the visual presentation.
[0005] Moreover, the proliferation of VR devices has led to a diverse range of form factors and capabilities. From standalone head-mounted displays (HMDs) to PC-based solutions and even mobile platforms, each type requires tailored approaches to optimize performance and ensure compatibility across various hardware configurations. Developers must consider how their software will run on different devices, accommodating variations in processing power, memory, and display characteristics. This diversity adds complexity to the development process, necessitating careful consideration of platform-specific requirements.
[0006] Furthermore, as VR experiences grow in scope and scale, so do the challenges related to data management. With increasing amounts of data being generated and shared among multiple participants, maintaining synchronization becomes crucial. Ensuring that all users see consistent information in real-time is essential for creating believable simulations and preventing anomalies that could detract from immersion. Achieving this consistency requires efficient communication protocols and robust mechanisms for handling latency, packet loss, and other connectivity issues.
[0007] In addition, the integration of artificial intelligence (AI) and machine learning (ML) techniques has opened up new possibilities for enhancing VR experiences. By analyzing user behavior and environmental data, AI-powered tools can adapt game mechanics, difficulty levels, and narrative paths in real-time, providing personalized engagement and dynamic storytelling. ML algorithms also enable advanced physics engines to simulate realistic behaviors, from complex material properties to intricate character movements. While these innovations offer immense potential, they introduce additional computational burdens and raise questions about how to effectively deploy these technologies without overwhelming hardware resources.
[0008] Beyond individual components, the evolution of VR has sparked discussions around social interaction and collaboration. Multiplayer experiences allow friends and strangers alike to share virtual worlds, fostering connections and communities built around common interests. To support large-scale group interactions, however, developers face the task of designing scalable architectures that handle increased load, reduce lag, and minimize disruptions caused by differing connection speeds. Balancing the needs of concurrent players while ensuring everyone has a responsive and enjoyable experience presents a significant engineering challenge.",
  "summary_of_invention": "[0010] Various embodiments disclosed herein relate to systems and methods for optimizing video streams in three-dimensional (3D), virtual environments. Some embodiments provide a computer-implemented method for managing video streams in 3D virtual environments. The method involves obtaining, at a server having at least one processor, audiovisual data. The audiovisual data is split into two separate components, namely, a video component and an audio component. The video component and the audio component are then separately transmitted to a client device. At the client device, the video component is received from the server and processed for rendering. Meanwhile, the audio component is played back independently of the video component. The playback of the video component is paused whenever the video component is outside of a current field of view (FOV). When the video component moves back into the FOV, the video component is re-enabled for playback. In some cases, the video component may be streamed at varying resolutions depending on the distance between the camera and the video component. For example, a higher resolution may be used when the video component is closer to the camera, whereas a lower resolution may be used when the video component is farther away from the camera. In yet other instances, the video component may be paused entirely until it returns to the FOV. Upon returning to the FOV, the video component may be restarted at a previously set resolution. Accordingly, the method ensures optimal use of available bandwidth while still providing a rich multimedia experience. Other embodiments relate to a system configured to perform the above-described method.",
