  "title": "VIRTUAL RAMP PREVIEW SYSTEM FOR VEHICLE",
  "technical_field": "[0002] This disclosure relates generally to vehicles and, more particularly, to a virtual ramp preview system for a vehicle.",
  "background_art": "[0003] Vehicles such as vans are often used to transport people who have mobility impairments, such as those requiring wheelchairs. For example, some people use power wheelchairs or manual wheelchairs while others may be confined to their bed and require a stretcher. In these cases, it can be difficult for them to enter or exit the van without assistance. To address this issue, many manufacturers offer vans equipped with deployable ramps. These ramps allow the person using the wheelchair to roll onto the van safely and easily.
[0004] However, deploying the ramp requires careful consideration to avoid accidents. There needs to be enough clearance around the ramp so that it does not hit any obstacles and also so that there is sufficient room to move into the van after rolling up the ramp. If the ramp hits something during deployment or if there isn't enough room to get inside the van, then the person could potentially fall off the ramp or become trapped underneath it. Furthermore, deploying the ramp requires coordination among multiple people to ensure safe operation. One person must open the door of the van, another person must lower the ramp, and yet another person must guide the person on the wheelchair along the ramp and into the van.
[0005] Additionally, when the ramp is extended, the van may need to remain stationary for a period of time until the ramp has been fully extended and the person has entered the van. During this time, other cars may pass by and try to cut through the gap left by the partially extended ramp. This creates safety risks for both the person trying to enter the van and for the drivers of the passing cars.
[0006] Thus, what is needed is a solution that helps reduce the risk associated with deploying the ramp and entering/ exiting the van. It would be advantageous to have a way to visualize how much space is available before deploying the ramp and entering/exiting the van. It would be beneficial to have a visual aid that shows where the ramp will extend relative to nearby obstacles and provides warnings if there aren't enough clearances. Further still, it would be desirable to have a system that allows the driver to see exactly where they are going and how far away they need to go to reach the van once the ramp is deployed.",
  "summary_of_invention": "[0010] Some aspects of the present technology relate to methods, systems, and devices for a virtual ramp-preview system for a vehicle. Aspects of the present technology include a virtual ramp-preview system for a vehicle. The system may include: a deployable ramp configured to deploy from a vehicle; one or more cameras coupled to an external surface of the vehicle and configured to provide one or mores fields of view of a surrounding area of the vehic- le; a user interface located within the vehicle and hav- ing a display; and an electronic control unit (EUC) elec- trically connected to the one or more cameras and the us- er interface and configured to: display, via the us- er interface, a composite image of the one or mores fields of view, the composite image including a virtu- al vehicle that is a virtual representation of the vehicle; and augment the composite image to include a virtual ram pextending from the virtual vehicle to assist a driver ofthe vehicle with determining an amount of space that is requi red to deploy the deployable ramp.
[0011] Another aspect of the present technology relates to a virtual ramp-preview system for a vehicle having a de- ployable wheelchair ramp. The system may include: one or more cameras located on an exterior surface of the vehicle and configured to provide real-time videodata includ- ing one or more fields of view of a surrounding areathat surrounds the vehicle; a user interface located within thevehicle and having a display; and an electronic controlunit (ECU) electrically connected to the onecameras and the user interface and configured to: gen- erate a composite image of the surrounding area of thevehi- cle based on the one or more fields of viewincluded in therereal-time video data, augment the composite imageto include a virtual ramp extending from a virtual ve- hicle, the virtual vehicle being a virtual representationof the vehicle, and display, via the user interfacetheaugmented composite image.
[0012] Yet another aspect of the present technology re- lates to a method for providing a virtual ramp-preview sys- tem for a vehicle. The method may include: gener- ating, via an electronic control unit (ECU): one or moreramp-preview images of a surrounding area of the vehiclebased on video data from one or more cameras; dis- playing, via a user interface coupled to the EUC, a com- posite image of the one or more composite images; aug- menting, via the ECU, the displayed compositemage toinclude a virtual ramp to assist a driver with esti- mating an amount of space that is required to deploy adeployable wheelchair ramp.
[0013] The foregoing general description and the fol- lowing detailed description are exemplary and explanatory only and are not restrictive of the invention, as defined in the appended claims.
[0014] The accompanying drawings, which are incorporated herein and form part of the specification, illustrate various embodiments of the present disclosure and, together with the description, explain the principles of the disclosure. The drawings are described below, and are presented for the purpose of illustrating related concepts and are not intended to show all features of the disclosure. The figures depicted in the drawing(s) are for illustration purposes only and, therefore, do not limit the scope of the disclosure. Other em- bodiments of the disclosure are possible.
FIG. 1 depicts a block diagram showing an ex- ample embodiment of a virtual ramp-preview system accor- ding to certain implementations;
