{
  "title": "METHOD FOR TRAINING AND USING A MACHINE MODEL FOR DISCOVERING OBJECTS IN AN IMAGE SEQUENCE",
  "technical_field": "The invention relates to the field of artificial intelligence, more specifically to the automatic processing of images.",
  "background_art": "[0002] In recent years, there has been a growing interest in deep neural networks for various applications involving image processing. These models can be used for example for classifying images [1], segmenting them [2], detecting objects [3], etc.
[0003] However, these models have several limitations related to their complexity, especially in terms of size, memory usage and computational resources required during inference. This is particularly true for tasks requiring real-time processing of video streams, where it would be desirable to obtain results as quickly as possible while consuming as few resources as possible.
[0004] To address this issue, techniques exist for reducing the size of deep neural networks while maintaining their performance, notably through pruning [4], quantification [5], knowledge distillation [6].
[0005] Another technique consists of training multiple instances of the same network architecture, but with different parameters. For example, two identical architectures may be learned simultaneously, one serving as a teacher (master model) and the other serving as a pupil (student model). During the training phase, the teacher model generates soft labels for the pupil model, allowing the latter to learn how to approximate the former's outputs without having to process large amounts of data. Once the student model has reached convergence, it is able to reproduce the behavior of the teacher model, thus obtaining similar performance with fewer parameters.
[0006] An alternative approach consists of training the student model by applying a series of intermediate checkpoints of the teacher model to the input data before feeding the final checkpoint of the teacher model to the student model. This allows the student model to learn not only the final outputs of the teacher model but also the intermediate representations generated along the way.
[0007] It appears however that even if the above methods allow a reduction in the number of parameters and/or computations performed by the model, they do not necessarily reduce the amount of memory needed for inference. Indeed, some operations require access to previously computed features, stored in memory, which limits the possibility of reducing the overall memory footprint of the model.
[0008] There are many ways to encode an image into a compact representation, including dimensionality reduction algorithms like PCA [7] or autoencoders [8]. More recently, attention mechanisms have emerged as a promising solution for efficient visual representation. Attention modules focus on specific parts of the input data, producing high-dimensional feature vectors that capture relevant information about the content of the input image.
[0009] Some solutions aim to improve the efficiency of attention mechanisms by reducing the spatial resolution of the input data. For instance, the authors in [9] propose a method for downsampling the input image while preserving the most important details. They achieve this by iteratively selecting the top-k pixels with highest activation values in each channel of the convolutional layer, then use bilinear interpolation to upsample the selected points to create a smaller feature map.
[0010] Other approaches rely on hierarchical attention structures, where higher-level features guide lower-level ones. For example, the authors in [10] introduce a multi-resolution attention mechanism that combines coarse-grained and fine-grained attention layers to effectively compress the input image. Similarly, the work presented in [11] proposes a pyramid attention structure that progressively refines the attention weights across different scales, leading to improved compression ratios.
[0011] Still another line of research focuses on improving the representational capacity of attention mechanisms themselves. For instance, the authors in [12] suggest replacing traditional dot-product attention with a learned attention weighting scheme, resulting in better performance under limited compute budgets. Additionally, the work described in [13] introduces a novel attention block design that incorporates both spatial and temporal dependencies within the attention calculation, yielding superior results compared to standard attention modules.
[0012] Despite these advancements, existing attention-based models often suffer from a fundamental limitation: they typically require significant computational resources to perform inference. Even though they may offer impressive reductions in memory requirements, their runtime costs remain substantial due to the extensive calculations involved in generating attention maps. As a result, deploying these models on resource-constrained devices remains challenging.
[0013] Furthermore, conventional attention-based models struggle to handle dynamic scenes efficiently. When dealing with sequences of images, they tend to focus heavily on static elements, neglecting moving objects and failing to provide accurate tracking over time. Moreover, their ability to detect new objects entering the scene is often poor, making them unsuitable for scenarios demanding robustness against changes in the environment.
[0014] Therefore, what is desired is a system capable of automatically identifying and localizing moving objects in videos while minimizing computational overhead and memory consumption.
[0015] One aspect of the present disclosure provides a computer-implemented method for training a machine model for discovering objects in an input image sequence (I), the model including an encoder (ENC, ENCEL) for coding each image into a first feature vector; an attentions module (ATT, ATTEL) configured to convert the first feature vector into a plurality of feature-vectors, named "slots" (S₁,... Sᵏ-1), with the state of a slot (Sk) being determined from a similarity computation between the first vector and each slot (Si) in its current state, with each similarity computation giving rise to an attention map (Wi, Wᵏ-1); a decoder (DEC) for decodifying all the slots (S₁,... Sk) in order to reconstruct an image sequence corresponding to I; the learning of the attention maps (Wi, Wk-1) being monitored by a set of boolean masks for discovering moving objects coming from an external source, named "pseudo-labelling", such that each attention map (Wi, Wk-1) is activated in a zone corresponding a particular object included in the pseudo-labellings (P), an additional attention map (Wb) being activated in a zone corresponding to the background of I; the pseudo-labellings (P) being filtered (FIL) by means a series of steps consisting of:
[0016] determining an attention map (Wf) of the foreground of I as being the negation of the attention map (Wb) of the background of I of the attention module (ATT, ATTEL)
[0017] calculating a trustworthiness score from the average of the values of the foreground attention map (Wf) at the position of each moving object present in a pseudo-labelling
[0018] filtering the moving objects of the pseudo-labellings for which the trustworthiness score is less than a given threshold.
[0019] According to another aspect, the method for training a machine model for discovering objects includes the step of determining the foreground attention map (Wf) from the background attention map (Wb) of the attention module (ATT, ATTEL).
[0020] According to another aspect, the method for discovering objects in an image sequence includes the following steps:
[0021] receiving an image sequence (I)
[0022] performing the machine model for discovering objects trained using the aforementioned method for the image sequence so as to produce at least one localization mask for an object in the image sequence, each localization mask being obtained from an attention map.
[0023] The disclosed subject matter will become more fully understood from the detailed description and the accompanying drawings, wherein:
[0024] FIG. 1 schematically illustrates a general diagram of the proposed architecture;
[0025] FIG. 2 shows a schematic view of a device implementing the proposed architecture;
[0026] FIG. 3 presents a flowchart illustrating the main stages of the method for training the model;
[0027] FIG. 4 depicts the different components of the attention module;
[0028] FIG. 5 represents the different components of the decoder;
[0029] FIG. 6 presents an illustration of the detection process;
[0030] FIG. 7 shows the visualization of the attention maps for the three frames of the test sequence;
[0031] FIG. 8 presents a graph showing the evolution of the precision-recall curve for different thresholds;
[0032] FIG. 9 presents a graph comparing the number of detected objects per frame for different architectures;
[0033] FIG. 10 presents a table summarizing the experimental results."
